/* book exam traps: A cold water spray is not a dryer: if the water is above the air dew point, it humidifies. Dehumidify by cooling below the dew point or with a desiccant. | Wet-bulb temperature and dew point are not the same. Wet bulb is reached by evaporative cooling; dew point is reached by cooling at constant Y. | Adiabatic humidification stops at saturation. Adding more spray does not raise Y beyond the saturation value at that wet-bulb temperature. | Steam injection adds water and heat together, so it is not the same as an adiabatic spray. | When cooling below the dew point, use Ysat at the final temperature for condensate, not at the initial dew point. */
