/* book: lead: A cooling tower is an evaporative cooler for water. Hot water falls through packing while air rises or crosses. A small fraction of the water evaporates and carries latent heat away, so the rest leaves colder. For large heat loads this is cheaper than a closed heat exchanger. Power stations, refineries, petrochemical plants and big air-conditioning systems use cooling towers to reuse cooling water */
