/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A solid sample has mass ms of dry solid and mw of water.
   - X is kg water per kg dry solid; mw is kg water; ms is kg dry solid.
   - w is kg water per kg wet solid; X is kg water per kg dry solid.
   - The reverse conversion is X = w/(1 − w).
   - Bound moisture is held by sorption on surfaces and inside cell walls; it exerts a vapour pressure below that of pure water.
   - Xf is free moisture, kg/kg dry solid; X is current moisture, kg/kg dry solid; X* is equilibrium moisture, kg/kg dry solid.
   - Equilibrium moisture content X* is the moisture a solid holds when it neither gains nor loses water to air at a fixed temperature and relative humidity.
   - Temperature shifts the isotherm: warmer air gives a lower X* at the same relative humidity.
   - Drying has three resistances in series: movement inside the solid, vapour transport away from the surface, and heat supply to the surface.
   - Early in drying the surface is wet.
*/
