/* book Worked example: Q: 100 kg of wet solid contains 30 kg of water. It is placed in air at 40 % relative humidity, for which the equilibrium moisture content is 0.10 kg/kg dry solid. Find the moisture on dry and wet bases, the free moisture and the mass of water that can be removed.
   1. Dry solid = 100 − 30 = 70 kg.
   2. Dry-basis moisture X = mw/ms = 30/70 = 0.4286 = 0.429 kg/kg dry solid.
   3. Wet-basis moisture = 30/100 = 0.30 = 30 %.
   4. Free moisture = X − X* = 0.4286 − 0.10 = 0.3286 = 0.329 kg/kg dry solid.
   5. Water left at equilibrium = X* × ms = 0.10 × 70 = 7.0 kg.
   6. Water removable = 30 − 7.0 = 23.0 kg, which also equals 0.3286 × 70 = 23.0 kg.
   Answer: Dry solid is 70 kg; X = 0.429 kg/kg dry solid; wet basis = 30 %; free moisture = 0.329 kg/kg dry solid; 23.0 kg of water can be removed, leaving 7.0 kg. */
